Falsifies `paging::aarch64::the_indices_and_offset_tile_the_address`.

The defect: the page offset is taken as thirteen bits, an 8 KiB granule. aarch64 really does
have 16 KiB and 64 KiB granules, so "how many bits is the offset" is a live question on this
ISA rather than a typo nobody would make; the rest of this crate is built on the 4 KiB granule
and a 512-entry table, and mixing the two gives a walk whose indices are all one bit high.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(reconstructed, va & 0x0000_ffff_ffff_ffff)`. The harness spells
the four shifts out as literals (39, 30, 21, 12) rather than asking the implementation for
them, which is what makes it able to catch this: a defect that moved the harness and the code
together would prove nothing, and this one cannot.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** The `sv39` record changes the stride between
levels and the `x86_64` one reverses the level numbering; all three are defects in the same
default method in `lib.rs`, caught by three ISAs' proofs.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -477,7 +477,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     /// covers the highest translated bits; each lower level shifts down by 9. Derived from
     /// [`LEVELS`](Self::LEVELS) so it is right for both a 4- and a 3-level walk.
     fn index(va: u64, level: usize) -> usize {
-        let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
+        let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 13;
         ((va >> shift) & 0x1ff) as usize
     }
 
